## Phase 1 — Domain and ingestion

### F1. Domain core

**Depends on:** F0.

**Files:** `src/Domain/src/Dispute/`

**What to do:**

An EventSauce `DisputeAggregate` plus value objects and a
`DisputeAggregateRepositoryInterface` (`retrieve` / `persist`, mirroring
`PaymentIntentAggregateRepositoryInterface`). No gateway code whatsoever.

```
DisputeAggregate (aggregate root)
  DisputeId              — UuidValueObject subclass, ours; never the provider's
  PaymentIntentId
  DisputeStage           — INQUIRY | CHARGEBACK | PRE_ARBITRATION | ARBITRATION
  DisputeStatus          — NEEDS_RESPONSE | UNDER_REVIEW | WON | LOST | ACCEPTED | EXPIRED | CLOSED
  SubmissionState        — NONE | DRAFT | UPLOAD_PENDING | SUBMITTED | CONFIRMED   (second axis, see below)
  DisputeReason          — { CardBrand, string rawCode, ReasonCategory }
  Money disputedAmount
  DisputeFee[]           — a list, never a single Money; see below
  ?\DateTimeImmutable deadlineAt
```

No history list. The case's history is a **projection of the event stream** — `DisputeOpened` for the
opening stage and every `DisputeStageChanged` after it, each carrying the provider's cycle code in
`providerCode`. Keeping a copy inside the aggregate would put the same fact in two places and make
the snapshot carry it into every write, for a value nothing in the aggregate reads. §0.2 already puts
history output on the projector side of the boundary.

No `GatewayAccountId` field — §0.4.

**Nor the provider's own name for the case — §0.4.** An earlier revision carried a
`GatewayDisputeRef` here (a string, because Nuvei's ids contain `/` and `+`) and threaded it through
`DisputeOpened`, `OpenDisputeCommand` and the port requests. Nothing in this tree works that way. The
provider's reference for one of our aggregates is a row in `gateway_references`, written and read
through `GatewayTransactionRepository`, and each adapter resolves it by our own id —
`CaptureAdapter:62-67`, and the same shape in `RefundAdapter`, `CancelAdapter` and `CreateAdapter`,
all of which throw rather than reach for a reference from anywhere else. **The aggregate's identity
is a uuid we mint**, and that is what it is for: ConnexPay re-references its cases (a second
chargeback carries a new `CaseNumber` inside the same `FamilyId`), and an aggregate identified by the
provider's name would change identity underneath its own event stream. Nothing is lost by the
absence — `DisputeId` is what the port requests carry (F2), `findForDispute()` is what the adapters
read, and `EloquentTransactionIdResolver::resolveDispute()` is how a resolution arriving under the
provider's own name finds the case it belongs to (F2). The idempotency key below is stated in terms
of what the guard actually compares.

**Fees are a list, not a field.** A single `Money disputeFee` contradicts A4, which requires the two
Stripe fees to stay distinct: "dispute *received*", debited at initiation and non-refundable outside
Mexico, and "dispute *countered*", returned on a win. ConnexPay has the same shape four times over —
$20 on the incoming chargeback, $20 on a retrieval request, $20 on an incoming pre-arbitration, $20
on reversal acceptance (a win), $0 on reversal denial. One dispute, several fees, charged at
different moments and moving in different directions. Collapsing them makes A4 unbuildable from what
Part I emits, which §Part II names as a Part I bug.

```php
DisputeFee  — { FeeType type, Money amount, \DateTimeImmutable chargedAt }
FeeType     — CHARGEBACK | RETRIEVAL | PRE_ARBITRATION | REVERSAL_ACCEPTANCE | COUNTERED
```

`amount` is the fee as charged and positive; direction is `type`'s business, and A4 decides the sign.
Where a provider states fees in a payload, the adapter that sees them records them
(`DisputeFeeRecorded`). ConnexPay states none in its field list, so there A4 derives them from
`NetPosition` and A5's settlement data — the aggregate does not guess. Re-observing a fee already
present (same `type`, `amount` and `chargedAt`) emits nothing.

**`SubmissionState` is a separate axis on purpose.** The provider's view of the case and our view of
our own submission attempt move independently: Nuvei sits in "file uploaded, callback not yet
received" while the provider still reports `NEEDS_RESPONSE`, and ConnexPay has an operator who
claims to have submitted while `HasResponse` is still `false`. Folding these into `DisputeStatus`
means F9 and A3 come back to rewrite F1's invariants. Two axes, each with its own transitions.

**Events:** `DisputeOpened`, `DisputeStageChanged`, `DisputeStatusChanged`, `DisputeDeadlineChanged`,
`DisputeFeeRecorded`, `EvidenceAttached`, `EvidenceSubmitted`, `EvidenceUploadConfirmed`,
`DisputeResolved`, `DisputeAccepted`.

**Invariants that must be covered by tests:**

- The `LOST → WON` transition **is allowed**. Stripe labels these as late wins; an issuer may credit
  outside the normal cycle. Test: aggregate in `LOST`, apply `DisputeResolved(WON)` — no exception.
- **Statuses move exactly like this, and these are the terminals** — anything not in the table throws:

  | From | To | Note |
  |---|---|---|
  | `NEEDS_RESPONSE` | `UNDER_REVIEW`, `WON`, `LOST`, `ACCEPTED`, `EXPIRED`, `CLOSED` | `EXPIRED` only on a provider signal or the application's expiry command |
  | `UNDER_REVIEW` | `WON`, `LOST`, `ACCEPTED`, `CLOSED` | |
  | `LOST` | `WON` | **late win** — the only permitted way out of `LOST` |
  | `WON` / `ACCEPTED` / `EXPIRED` / `CLOSED` | — | terminal, no transition out |

  `EXPIRED` is only reachable from a provider signal or from an explicit expiry command issued by the
  application's clock. The aggregate has no timer of its own.
- **`ACCEPTED` is not overwritten by the `lost` the provider reports afterwards.** F7's
  `POST /v1/disputes/:id/close` moves the dispute to `lost` (the API does not know we conceded
  deliberately, only that we stopped fighting). Our status records *why* — so the close emits
  `DisputeAccepted` → `ACCEPTED`, and a later `charge.dispute.closed` carrying `lost` finds the
  aggregate already terminal and emits nothing. A4 reads `ACCEPTED` as loss recognition. Test both
  halves: the close reaching `ACCEPTED`, and the subsequent `lost` changing nothing.
- **`ARBITRATION` has no producer.** Stripe does not support the phase; Nuvei's highest code in the
  reference is `IPA` (pre-arbitration); ConnexPay's `CaseType` table tops out at pre-arbitration (9
  and 24). The value stays in `DisputeStage` because the networks have the phase and D1 may reach it,
  but **no adapter maps to it and no invariant may assume it is reachable**. A provider signal that
  would land there is an unmapped-case error surfaced to the operator, not a silent default.
- **`SubmissionState` moves like this** — the second axis, independent of the first:

  | From | To | Trigger |
  |---|---|---|
  | `NONE` | `DRAFT` | evidence staged at the provider (Stripe `submit: false`) |
  | `NONE` | `SUBMITTED` | operator marks a portal submission (ConnexPay only) |
  | `DRAFT` | `SUBMITTED` | the submission call goes out |
  | `DRAFT` | `UPLOAD_PENDING` | file uploaded, awaiting the provider's confirmation |
  | `UPLOAD_PENDING` | `SUBMITTED` | upload confirmed, submission sent (F8 step 3) |
  | `UPLOAD_PENDING` | `DRAFT` | the re-query shows the file was never accepted |
  | `SUBMITTED` | `CONFIRMED` | the provider acknowledges the response — Nuvei's Image or Action callback, ConnexPay's `HasResponse: true` |

  `CONFIRMED` is terminal and is **never synthesised**. Where a provider gives no acknowledgement
  signal — Stripe does not — the state stops at `SUBMITTED` and stays there. `HasResponse: false` on a
  later poll leaves `SUBMITTED` untouched; it does not demote it. Both axes are recorded on the
  aggregate, but a change in one never implies a change in the other.
- `DisputeReason` stores the raw code **and** the `CardBrand` **and** the category. Mapping raw code
  to category lives in a separate table-driven `ReasonCodeMapper` under `ValueObject/`. Visa `13.1`,
  Mastercard `4853` and Amex `C08` do not collapse into one enum: the hint logic hangs off the
  (brand, raw code) pair, and a code one brand does not issue must not become answerable through
  another brand's entry.
  Note what that argument is and is not — it is about **distinct codes never merging**, not about
  every pair earning its own requirement set. F6's table is the authority on requirement sets, and
  it deliberately gives `visa:13.1`, `mastercard:4853` and `amex:C08` the **same** set: "goods or
  services not received" is one question asked by three networks. A pair earns a different set by
  being a different *question*, not a different code. Do not "fix" the table by making every key
  unique.
- The deadline is a plain `\DateTimeImmutable` and nothing on it says where it came from. An earlier
  revision carried a `DeadlineAt` value object with an `isAuthoritative` flag; it is gone because no
  rule read it — escalation fires at a fixed offset from whatever date is stored, and the application
  that applies a buffer knows it applied one. See the revision block at the top.
- Idempotency: re-applying the same external signal produces no new domain event. The key is
  `(GatewayId, DisputeId, providerEventKey)` — `GatewayId` from the message header, `DisputeId` the
  case's own identity, `providerEventKey` defined **per provider**:
  - Stripe — the webhook `event.id` (already `ParsedEvent::$externalId`).
  - Nuvei — `DisputeEventId`.
  - ConnexPay — **there is no event id.** The key is a hash of the case's normalised state snapshot,
    computed by the adapter in F5 against the contract below.
  **What the guard compares is narrower than that triple, and deliberately.**
  `DisputeSignal` carries the provider's own key alone, and the aggregate compares it against the
  **most recent** key it applied (`hasAlreadyApplied()` — one remembered value, never a set).
  Neither `DisputeId` nor `GatewayId` is a component of the compared string: `DisputeId` is the
  identity of the stream the comparison happens inside, which is what makes one component
  sufficient, and `GatewayId` is a property of that stream too — a case is raised at one provider
  and belongs to one gateway account for its whole life, so a gateway component could never tell two
  keys inside one stream apart, while prefixing the key with it did write our account identity into
  the event trail, since `signal_key` is recorded on every dispute event. The recorder is handed the
  gateway as an argument and picks the stream with it, and `gateway_references.gateway_id` records it
  durably against the case. Two aggregates may hold the same `providerEventKey` without colliding,
  because a signal is only ever applied to the case the delivery resolved to.
  **No component may be empty.** A blank `providerEventKey` is a malformed delivery or a mapping
  bug: reject it as an error and surface it. It must never act as a wildcard matching every case, and
  never as a constant that makes the second delivery look identical to the first. The reference is
  not a component and cannot stand in as an empty one — see the field list above.
- **The ConnexPay hashing contract** (F5 implements it verbatim; do not improvise a second one):
  - Fields, in this order: `FamilyId`, `CaseNumber`, `CaseType`, `ReasonCode`, `ResolutionTo`,
    `WinLoss`, `HasResponse`, `HasImage`, `IsSurrendered`, `DueDate`, `NetPosition`.
  - Omission is meaningful: a field that is absent or `null` contributes the literal token `-`, and
    so does an empty string. Absent and empty must not be distinguishable — the CMS API returns both
    for the same underlying "not set".
  - Per-field normalisation: dates as `Y-m-d` in UTC; amounts as integer minor units in the currency
    the payload states; codes and enums as the raw provider value, case preserved, untrimmed.
  - Serialise as `field=value` pairs joined by `\n` in the order above, then `sha256`, prefixed
    `v1:`. If the field list ever changes, bump to `v2:` — a silent re-hash would make every open
    case look new.
  - The hash is of **the case, not the delivery**: `GetByUser` and `GetByResolvedDate` return the
    same case in different shapes and must produce the same hash.
  - **Where the hasher is implemented, and why `Domain` cannot hold it.** It is F5's own code, at
    `src/ConnexPay/src/Dispute/ProviderSnapshotCanonicaliser.php`, beside the field list
    `CaseSnapshot::hashFields()` assembles. A provider package reaches `['Common', 'Gateway']` and
    nothing else (§0.4, `tests/Arch/PackageHierarchyTest.php`), so a hasher published under
    `Domain\Dispute\*` is unreachable from `CaseDiffer`, `CaseSnapshot` and `DisputePoller`: the
    contract would sit where its only caller is forbidden to look, and the Arch suite is what says
    so. `Common` and `Gateway` are not right either — ConnexPay is the one provider with no event id
    (Stripe has `event.id`, Nuvei has `DisputeEventId`), so a shared home would be a cross-provider
    abstraction with a single consumer. **The aggregate is promised nothing about the hash's
    internals**: it treats `providerEventKey` as opaque and only ever compares it, so what the hasher
    owes is one stable key per case and a different key when the case changes — pinned by tests in
    the package that computes it.
- **Compare against the previous snapshot, not against a set of hashes ever seen.** F5 stores, per
  `CaseNumber`, the **single most recent** hash and compares the new one to it; an unchanged case
  emits nothing. A *set* of seen hashes would be a defect: any case that returns to an earlier
  combination of values revisits an earlier hash, and a set suppresses that transition as a
  duplicate. Keeping only the previous value makes such a return a change — which it is, and which is
  precisely the transition the `HasResponse` and `WinLoss` mappings exist to report.

**Done when:** aggregate unit tests are green under `--testsuite=Domain`, including every invariant
above and at least one test per `providerEventKey` shape. Not a single line of gateway code.

