### F2. Ports and the action model

**Depends on:** F1.

**Files:** `src/Domain/src/Dispute/Port/`, `src/Domain/src/Dispute/Port/Request/` for the ports and
their requests; `src/Domain/src/Dispute/Contract/` for the action interface and its visitor, and
`src/Domain/src/Dispute/ValueObject/` for the three concretes and the set. The split is the ruling
itself: **an action is a Domain value object, not a port type**, and this tree's visitor precedent
divides the same way (`Common/src/Contract/Challenge.php` beside
`Common/src/ValueObject/Challenge/RedirectChallenge.php` — ten `ValueObject` directories hold zero
interfaces).

**What to do:**

Ports in the house shape — Request in, Outcome out. The action model is **one value object per
action behind an interface**, with the concrete type recovered by double dispatch — not one property
bag with a `kind()`:

```php
// src/Domain/src/Dispute/Contract/
interface DisputeAction
{
    /**
     * @template TReturn
     * @param  DisputeActionVisitor<TReturn>  $visitor
     * @return TReturn
     */
    public function accept(DisputeActionVisitor $visitor): mixed;
}

/** @template TReturn */
interface DisputeActionVisitor
{
    /** @return TReturn */
    public function visitRespond(RespondDisputeAction $action): mixed;

    /** @return TReturn */
    public function visitAccept(AcceptDisputeAction $action): mixed;

    /** @return TReturn */
    public function visitDashboard(DashboardDisputeAction $action): mixed;
}

// src/Domain/src/Dispute/ValueObject/ — final readonly, each with a bare #[Override] accept()
// that calls its own visit method; implementations carry no docblock (RedirectChallenge.php:35-39)
final readonly class RespondDisputeAction implements DisputeAction
{
    public function __construct(
        public ?EvidenceRequirements $requirements,   // the live read's pair, or nothing
        public \DateTimeImmutable $respondBy,
    ) {}
}

final readonly class AcceptDisputeAction implements DisputeAction
{
    public function __construct(
        public Money $disputedAmount,                 // the ceiling, not the concession
        public \DateTimeImmutable $respondBy,
    ) {}
}

final readonly class DashboardDisputeAction implements DisputeAction
{
    /** Null when we hold no portal base for this provider; the case is surfaced either way. */
    public function __construct(public ?string $dashboardUrl) {}
}

interface DisputeActionsPort
{
    public function availableActions(AvailableActionsRequest $request): DisputeActionSet;
}

interface SubmitEvidencePort
{
    public function submit(SubmitEvidenceRequest $request): SubmissionOutcome;
}

interface AcceptDisputePort
{
    /** Partial amount, when the provider supports it, rides on the request. */
    public function accept(AcceptDisputeRequest $request): AcceptOutcome;
}
```

`DisputeActionSet` moves from `Port/` to `ValueObject/` with the class-string keys: it holds at most
one action per concrete class, so `has(RespondDisputeAction::class)` and
`get(AcceptDisputeAction::class)` are psalm-typed and no enum is consulted anywhere. **There is no
`ActionType` and no `Execution`** — "who performs this" is which class you are holding, answered by
the visitor, and `ActionType::AcceptPartial` is gone because partiality is the sum the *call* states
(`AcceptDisputeRequest::$partialAmount`), not a property of the action.

The template on `accept()` is load-bearing and must be copied from `Common/src/Contract/Challenge.php`
verbatim, bound parameter included. Without `@param DisputeActionVisitor<TReturn>` the type argument
is unbound, psalm resolves it to `mixed`, and at `errorLevel="3"` the whole `MIXED_ISSUES` family is
switched off (`Psalm/Config.php:1804-1809`), so the degradation is **silent**: `DisputeActionVisitor`
ships, and a per-gateway implementation of it does not — no gateway in this tree holds a capability
that is a strict subset of `{Respond, Accept}`.

Three port interfaces rather than one, because no provider implements all three the same way:
ConnexPay has no API submission at all and would be forced to stub two methods it can never honour.
This is the same reason `PaymentIntent` has `CapturePort`, `CancelPort` and `RefundPort` separately.

**Every request names the case by our own id, with one argued exception.**
`AcceptDisputeRequest` and `SubmitEvidenceRequest` carry a `DisputeId` and none of the provider's
names for the case: the adapter resolves the reference from `gateway_references` through
`findForDispute()` — the `CaptureAdapter` shape — and an adapter that finds no row for a case of ours
**throws** (`RuntimeException`) rather than falling back to a reference read off a request, because a
missing row is our own bookkeeping and not the provider's answer. The exception is
`AvailableActionsRequest`, which takes one of two named constructors rather than a nullable field
pair, so a half-named case cannot be built:

- `ofOurs(DisputeId $disputeId)` — a case of ours; the adapter resolves the provider's reference
  itself, and the id is also how it reads our own state (a conceded case reports `lost` at the
  provider, which is a non-empty answer there and an empty one here).
- `unattributable(string $providerReference)` — a case we hold no aggregate for: no `DisputeId`, no
  row keyed by one, and therefore no name but the provider's own (`CaseNumber` at ConnexPay). A blank
  reference is refused at construction, as every blank reference in this domain is.

**That second constructor is the only place a provider reference appears in the Domain's
vocabulary, and it is passed through and never stored.** It exists because there is a real path on
which a case we cannot resolve to an id of ours still has to be shown to somebody: resolution depends
on a `gateway_references` row that A0 writes and that later calls overwrite, and
`GatewayDisputeRecorder::onUnmatchedDispute()` (F5) is a producer of exactly those cases — a
ConnexPay case whose `SaleGuid` names no payment of ours. What that constructor is **not** is a
licence for a request that usually carries an id of ours to carry a provider's name as well, which is
what the earlier revision's nullable `?DisputeId` invited; nor is it justified by the unsourced
premise that `OrderNumber` may be empty (F5 states what that premise actually is).

`AcceptOutcome` is not `void`: it carries what the provider did (accepted in full / accepted
partially / already closed) so the aggregate records a fact rather than an assumption.

**Critical requirement:** `availableActions` must **not** return an empty set merely because the
provider offers no API action. An empty set means "this dispute isn't waiting on us": won, lost,
accepted, expired, or challenging is disallowed by the rules.

When an action is possible but a human performs it, return a `DashboardDisputeAction` carrying the
deep link into the provider's portal — and **nothing else**. This applies to all of ConnexPay. The
link is nullable, which is a fact rather than a degradation: `ConnexPayDisputePortalLink` is the only
producer of an operator URL in the tree and its configuration key
(`services.connexpay.dispute_portal_url`) is unset, and no Nuvei channel has a portal-link producer
at all. The operator knows where the provider's panel is; the action says so, and the case is
surfaced with or without a URL. **This deliberately retires the old
`Execution::Operator ⇒ externalUrl !== null` invariant**, recorded as a reversal with reasons.

**The rule that fills the set is not here — it is `DisputeAggregate::availableDisputeActions()`**
(F1). It is a pure Domain query over the case's own stage, status and deadline, and it is the
**ceiling**: `respond` when the status is `NeedsResponse` and a deadline is known, `accept` on top of
that at every stage but `Inquiry`, with `disputedAmount()` supplying `AcceptDisputeAction`'s sum and
the pair the live read states supplying `RespondDisputeAction`'s requirements. A provider adapter
**may only subtract** from that answer. The two arguments' asymmetry is deliberate: a `respondBy`
passed in wins over the aggregate's own and null means "the read stated none", because
`deadlineAt()` is kept current by `changeDeadline()`; the evidence pair is **not** taken from the
record as a fallback, because `reason()` is written once by `open()` (`DisputeAggregate.php:343`) and
never moved, so the record would answer for the opening delivery's pair. Write the reasons for both
into the aggregate's docblock.

**Also here:** the cross-gateway recorder contract, in the Gateway package, next to its siblings:

```
src/Gateway/src/Webhook/Recorder/GatewayDisputeRecorder.php
```

```php
interface GatewayDisputeRecorder
{
    /** A case we can attach to a PaymentIntent. `$snapshot` always carries the gateway dispute ref. */
    public function onDisputeObserved(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $paymentIntentId, DisputeSnapshot $snapshot): RecorderOutcome;

    /** A resolution, keyed on the provider's reference — a resolution may arrive with no
     *  PaymentIntent in it. The snapshot's gateway dispute ref is what ties the two calls together. */
    public function onDisputeResolved(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $disputeRef, DisputeResolution $resolution): RecorderOutcome;

    /** ConnexPay only: a case we could not attach to any PaymentIntent. */
    public function onUnmatchedDispute(GatewayId $gatewayId, UnmatchedDispute $case): RecorderOutcome;
}
```

**Why the first two are keyed differently.** `onDisputeObserved` is reached *through* the
PaymentIntent — the resolver found one — so the PaymentIntent is its key and the provider's reference
rides inside the snapshot. `onDisputeResolved` cannot be: a resolution may arrive for a case whose
PaymentIntent never resolved, or after a backlog import, so the reference itself has to be the key.
**`DisputeSnapshot` must therefore carry the gateway dispute reference as a required field** — it is
the only thing that ties the two calls to the same dispute, and without it the resolution lands on
nothing.

**That field belongs to a DTO at the ingestion boundary, not to the aggregate.** `DisputeSnapshot`
lives in `src/Gateway/`, which is where a provider's name for a case legitimately appears: the
delivery carries it, the resolver has not yet found an aggregate for it, and
`RecorderOutcome::NotFound` is how an implementation says "not observed yet". What the Domain never
sees is the reference — the aggregate holds `DisputeId` and `PaymentIntentId` (F1) and nothing of the
provider's. The implementation that maps one onto the other is also the one place holding both halves
at once, which is why writing the `gateway_references` row is A0's rather than the aggregate's
(below).

**Where those types live, and what they may carry.** `DisputeSnapshot`, `DisputeResolution` and
`UnmatchedDispute` are declared next to this interface in `src/Gateway/src/Webhook/Recorder/`, as
**flat DTOs of scalars, enums, `Money` and `\DateTimeImmutable`** — the types `GatewayFeeRecorder`
already uses. No `Domain\Dispute\*` value object crosses into the Gateway package; the
"Gateway stays free of domain VOs" convention (§0.3, `TransactionIdResolver` docblock) holds. They
carry everything F3–F5 learn — stage, status, the deadline the provider stated, fees, provider codes — precisely
so F1's aggregate can be created straight into a terminal state. Mapping the flat DTO onto domain VOs
happens *inside* the implementation, in the application or in `src/Laravel/`.

**Dispute-reference storage — what makes the lookups above possible.** The mapping between our id and
the provider's name for a case is a row in the polymorphic `gateway_references` table, whose shape is
`(gateway_id, referenceable_type, referenceable_id) → reference`. So:

- add `TYPE_DISPUTE = 'dispute'` next to `TYPE_PAYMENT_INTENT` / `TYPE_REFUND` in
  `EloquentGatewayTransactionRepository`;
- add the pair `findForDispute(string $disputeId): ?string` and
  `saveForDispute(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $disputeId, string $reference): void` to
  `Gateway\Contract\GatewayTransactionRepository`, mirroring `findForRefund` / `saveForRefund`
  exactly. This is the one the four dispute port adapters depend on, and it is deliberately on the
  Gateway contract rather than on the Domain side: it is provider bookkeeping, and the Domain's
  vocabulary does not name a provider reference at all (F1);
- add `resolveDispute(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $disputeRef): ?string` to
  `EloquentTransactionIdResolver`, parallel to the two methods already there — the reverse direction,
  for a resolution that arrives addressed by the provider's own reference rather than by our id.

**The row is read in both directions and written in one.** By our id, from the port adapters; by the
provider's reference, from `resolveDispute()`. It is written by A0's `GatewayDisputeRecorder`
implementation when a case is observed — and **nothing implements that interface yet**: A0 is
unwritten, so today no dispute-reference row is ever written and both lookups return `null`. That is
the honest state of it. F2 declares the storage and the types, F3/F4/F5 produce the snapshots, and
the write is Part II's.

**No migration and no model are needed**, which is why this does not collide with rule 7:
`referenceable_type` is a plain `string` column with no enum constraint and no morph map is
registered, and `referenceable_id` is a uuid — our `DisputeId` is one. Writing the row is A0's job,
since the recorder implementation is the one place holding both halves at once: the aggregate holds
our id and the observation carries the provider's, and nothing else in the tree holds both. One trap:
never call `$row->referenceable()` on a dispute row — with no morph map entry `MorphTo` will look for
a class named after the type and fail. The resolver queries the table directly and does not.

**Fraud reports get their own recorder, not this one.** F4's `Fraud reported Transaction` (TC40) is
not a dispute and must not reach `Dispute`. It needs its own interface in the same directory —
`GatewayFraudReportRecorder` — because A6's VAMP numerator reads it independently of any dispute.
Declaring it here is what makes F4 buildable.

**Done when:** interfaces exist plus an in-memory fake for tests; the `TYPE_DISPUTE` row round-trips
in **both** directions — by our id through `findForDispute()`, by the provider's reference through
`resolveDispute()`, with the row written by the test itself because nothing implements the recorder
yet; and a test asserts that a fake ConnexPay-like adapter returns a non-empty set carrying a
`DashboardDisputeAction` and a working deep link — the non-emptiness is the assertion, since an empty
set here would be the false statement that the case is not waiting on us.

