### F5. ConnexPay: ingestion

**Depends on:** F2.

**Files:** `src/ConnexPay/src/ConnexPayDisputesClient.php`,
`src/ConnexPay/src/Dispute/{CaseDiffer,CaseSnapshot,DisputePoller,ProviderSnapshotCanonicaliser}.php`

**What to do:** a client and a **pollable service** — not a scheduled job. The schedule is A0's; this
package exposes `poll(PollWindow): PollResult` and the application decides when to call it.

**The client is a third one.** `ConnexPayClient` and `ConnexPayPurchasesClient` are already separate
because they differ in base URL and auth (`ConnexPayPurchasesClient.php:15`); the CMS dispute API
differs again — base `https://cmsapi.connexpay.com/`, Basic auth, **credentials separate from the
regular CRM user**. Note also that `ConnexPayHttpClientInterface` declares only `post()` and `put()`;
the dispute API is read-only, so either extend the interface with `get()` or give the new client its
own narrow interface. Prefer the latter — the existing two implementations have no use for `get()`.

**Two independent cursors, both required:**

```
GetByUser?startDate=&endDate=            → new and updated cases
GetByResolvedDate?startDate=&endDate=    → resolved cases
```

A case created six weeks ago can resolve today and won't show up in the first query. Skipping the
second cursor produces the classic bug where disputes stay open forever on your side while the
provider has already closed them. Cursor **storage** is the application's (A0); `poll()` takes the
window and returns the new one.

**Deduplication without event IDs.** ConnexPay returns a state snapshot. The differ hashes the
normalised snapshot per `CaseNumber` using the contract fixed in F1, compares against **the single
most recent hash** handed to it by the caller, and emits recorder calls only from the diff. One hash
per case, not a set of hashes ever seen — F1 explains what a set loses. This path is a poll, not a
webhook delivery, so it does not go through the webhook idempotency store and the empty-`external_id`
hazard there does not apply to it; `PollResult` carries the new hashes back for the caller to store
next to the cursor.

**Mapping:**

| ConnexPay | Domain |
|---|---|
| `FamilyId` | the provider's own reference for the family — what groups a first chargeback, its representments and its reversals; carried as the snapshot's `familyRef` so a recorder routes a later cycle to the aggregate the first one opened, **not** because the aggregate is identified by it |
| `CaseNumber` | key of an individual case-cycle event within the family |
| `SaleGuid` | link to the PaymentIntent — **primary** matching key; the guid the `gateway_references` row holds once the sale is confirmed |
| `OrderNumber` | second matching key — our own PaymentIntent id, in the row only between the hosted-page request and the sale webhook that overwrites it |
| `Arn` | last, shared across all cases of a transaction — **not a working fallback here**: nothing in this repository stores one |
| `CaseType` | stage — table in the reference, use it verbatim |
| `ResolutionTo` | `M` → waiting on us; `B` → waiting on the bank; `S` → split; `G` → moved to GL |
| `DueDate` | `deadlineAt` |
| `HasResponse` | confirmation that a response landed — drives `SubmissionState::CONFIRMED` |
| `NetPosition` | source of truth for money in A4 |
| `WinLoss` | derived, display only |
| `CardBrand` (1–5) | `Common\ValueObject\CardBrand` |

**`DisputeStatus` has no source in this table yet, and closing that is part of this task.** F1's
`open()` requires a status, and none of the three position fields above produces one: `CaseType`
gives the stage, `ResolutionTo` gives who is waiting, and `WinLoss` is dismissed in the table above
as display-only — so nothing here says whether a newly observed case is `NEEDS_RESPONSE` or already
`UNDER_REVIEW`, or whether a case gone quiet under review is `WON` / `LOST`. Deriving it from
`ResolutionTo` together with `WinLoss` is the obvious candidate (`M`, waiting on us →
`NEEDS_RESPONSE`; a win/loss outcome → `WON` / `LOST`), but that is **provider semantics and must be
confirmed against ConnexPay's own documentation before it is written**, exactly as this plan treats
every other provider-code claim. A mapping invented from the field names alone is the confident
silence the rest of this document keeps refusing, and it lands on the aggregate's most consequential
axis. Carry both raw fields on the snapshot — F2's DTO does — and write the mapping here, once, for
F5 and F9 to share.

**These tables live here, once.** `CaseType → DisputeStage` (reference) and `ResolutionTo →
waiting-on` are needed by both this task and F9, and F9 must consume what is declared here rather
than restate it — two independent copies of the same provider semantics inside one package drift
apart, and nothing fails loudly when they do. F9 therefore depends on F5, not only on F6.

**Matching: `SaleGuid` first, then `OrderNumber`, then `Arn` — and the order is a fact about our own
table, not a preference.** All three go through the same `TransactionIdResolver`, and each one's
standing is different:

1. **`SaleGuid` — primary, and the one that ordinarily resolves.** It is ConnexPay's own guid for the
   sale the case is against — the value the case payload documents — and it is exactly what the
   `gateway_references` row holds once the sale is confirmed, for both a card-data sale (the guid the
   sales API returns synchronously) and a hosted-page sale (the guid the sale webhook carries).
   `TransactionIdResolver::resolvePaymentIntent()` matches `gateway_references.reference` **exactly**
   (`EloquentTransactionIdResolver:22-29`): it does not recognise our aggregate id, and no row stores
   a `CaseNumber`.
2. **`OrderNumber` — second, and narrower than it looks.** It is *our* PaymentIntent id, sent as
   `clientUniqueId`, so `resolvePaymentIntent()` can match it only while the row still holds it:
   between the hosted-page request (`CreateAdapter:80` stores `$result->reference`, and for the hosted
   page that reference *is* our `OrderNumber` — ConnexPay returns no guid, because the sale does not
   exist until somebody pays) and the sale webhook, which **overwrites that same row** with the sale
   guid (`SaleApprovedHandler` → `onGatewaySuccess(..., $saleGuid, ...)` →
   `EloquentPaymentIntentRecorder:94` → `saveForPaymentIntent`). After the sale lands, the row no
   longer answers to the order number.
3. **`Arn` — last, and not a working fallback.** Nothing in this repository ever stores an `Arn`: a
   grep finds the word only inside this dispute domain, so the branch resolves only if an application
   outside this tree happened to store one against a payment intent of ours. It is tried because the
   payload carries the field and because a lookup costs nothing, and an operator reading an unmatched
   case should see it — not because anything here can be expected to match on it. A test asserting
   that it resolves would be asserting a row this repository never writes.

`CaseSnapshot` reads `SaleGuid` as a nullable field and deliberately **not** into `hashFields()`: the
hash is a change detector over the case's state, and the guid is stable identity (F1). `UnmatchedDispute`
gains a nullable `saleGuid` in the same change, so a case that matched nothing still hands the
operator the one reference on it that can be resolved back to a payment.

**Unmatched cases.** This section previously stated as fact that `OrderNumber` "may be empty, may
belong to another system, and `Arn` may be absent on early events". **That premise is not sourced and
is now carried as a premise pending captured payloads.** The only payloads any test here has seen are
hand-written from ConnexPay's documented field list and its own sample response, and the package's own
test docblock says exactly that — "**None of it was recorded from ConnexPay.** F0 replaces them in
place with real captured payloads". What follows from the order above does not depend on the premise,
and is the reading this document now uses: **with `SaleGuid` tried first, a case that matches nothing
is one whose guid names no payment of ours** — a sale we never processed, not a field the provider
left out. So a case that matches none of the three is **not** dropped and **not** retried forever: it
goes out through `GatewayDisputeRecorder::onUnmatchedDispute()` with everything we know and its
snapshot hash is stored like any other, so the next poll doesn't re-emit it. The requests an
operator's screen is then built from are `AvailableActionsRequest::unattributable($providerReference)`
(F2) — a case with no aggregate of ours has no id to be named by, and this queue is where such a case
comes from. Without this the poller silently loses cases, which is the same failure mode as the
missing second cursor and just as hard to notice.

**The overwrite on capture is why this works, not a hazard to it — and that is settled from our own
code, not left open.** `EloquentGatewayTransactionRepository::save()` overwrites `reference` on
transition while merging `metadata`, and a ConnexPay capture does return a reference
(`Capture::capture()` — `outcome($response['sale'] ?? $response)`, with the sale's guids, not the
capture's, being what later Returns and Voids expect — through
`MapsConnexPayOutcome::outcome()`'s `successReference()`), which `CaptureAdapter:79-80` writes back.
The row's `reference` is therefore replaced on capture — **with the settled sale's reference, and a
chargeback is raised against the settled transaction**, so the value left in the row is exactly the
one the case names. The order above depends on that, and the tree already documents where the
*opening* reference belongs instead: `MapsConnexPayOutcome::openingMetadata()` keeps it in
`metadata['opening_transaction_reference']` precisely because "`reference` overwrites on transition,
so once a capture lands the row holds the settle reference" — and `metadata` is merged, never
replaced. No durable home needs inventing; one exists for the reference that does move. What this
settles on its own is the card-data sale, where no separate capture happens and the row holds the
guid the sale itself named; the auth-only path is what the next paragraph leaves to a live check.

**What is still open is narrower, and belongs to a live-API check.** Whether the guid a capture
returns for an **auth-only** payment is the same string a case names as `SaleGuid` — i.e. whether the
settled reference and the case's guid are the same value for that path — and how `PartialCapture`
relates to it. A card-data sale takes no separate capture, so the question does not arise for it. The
repository has env-gated live integration tests since commit `3938c18`; that is where it is answered,
and it is not to be inferred from the documentation here.

**Also here:** retries with exponential backoff, a cap on cycle duration, and a `lastSuccessfulPoll`
timestamp on `PollResult` so the application can alarm on staleness. If the poller dies quietly
nobody notices — unlike webhooks, where delivery fails visibly on the provider's side.

**Done when:** ConnexPay contract tests are green; a diff test passes (re-running with the same
payload emits nothing); **the matching test that matters is that a case whose `SaleGuid` resolves
reaches `onDisputeObserved()` rather than the unmatched queue** — that is the case the old
`OrderNumber`-first order sent to the operator by mistake, so it is the one that has to be pinned.
The two later keys get narrower assertions: that `OrderNumber` is tried after `SaleGuid`, and that a
case only the `Arn` branch could have identified **lands unmatched** unless something outside this
repository stored one — an `Arn` test that asserts resolution would be asserting a row nothing in
this tree writes. Plus the unmatched path itself, asserting that the `UnmatchedDispute` handed to the
recorder carries the case's `SaleGuid`.

