### F9. ConnexPay: operator handoff

**Depends on:** F6, F7 for the `ReadsDisputeCases` role and its command/reading types (consume them,
do not invent a second read shape), and F5 for the shared `CaseType` / `ResolutionTo` mapping —
consume it, do not restate it.

**Files:** `src/ConnexPay/src/Dispute/` (the CMS read behind a `ReadsDisputeCases` implementation on
`ConnexPayGateway`), `src/Laravel/src/Port/` for the `DisputeActionsPort` implementation (§0.4:
`src/ConnexPay/` cannot name `DisputeAction`, `DisputeActionSet` or `DashboardDisputeAction`), and
`src/Laravel/src/Dispute/` for the
deep link and the evidence composition — **not** `src/ConnexPay/`, whatever the earlier revision of
this line said; see the revision note.
**No new role interface**: F7 already declared `Gateway\Role\ReadsDisputeCases` (with
`Command\DisputeCaseQuery` and `Contract\DisputeCaseReading`) and there is no role for ConnexPay to
add — it has no API submission and no API concession, so both of its portal actions are operator
actions. Reuse F5's `ConnexPayDisputesClientInterface` for the read; it is already the one place the
CMS host and Basic-auth live.

**What to do:** implement `DisputeActionsPort` (in `src/Laravel/src/Port/`, returning a
`DashboardDisputeAction` carrying a deep link into CMS), and the PDF package builder matching their
requirements: legible, large enough, black-and-white print, portrait orientation. The portal accepts
PDF and JPEG.

**The substitution is the one place in the model that is neither a ceiling nor a subtraction, and
both docblocks must say so rather than implying a subtraction.** This adapter consults the case's own
answer for whether anything is left on it, and then travels an action of its own: the link, in place
of the API call the case named. Two consequences follow and both must be pinned by tests rather than
left implicit — the concession is **dropped** even where the case admits one (ConnexPay's CMS API has
no call to close a case, no payload states whether the portal would take one, and offering an
irreversible act on a guess is the same class of mistake as guessing the portal's hostname), and the
link is **offered on an inquiry**, where the case admits a response and no concession, because a
response is work for a human and nothing about the substitution depends on *which* actions the case
named — only on whether it named any.

**And the deadline and the template are lost here, deliberately.** A `DashboardDisputeAction` carries
the link and nothing else (the owner's ruling), so an **unattributable** ConnexPay case answers a link
alone while the response window runs — an attributable one can still read `deadlineAt()` from its own
aggregate. Recorded with the reason rather than left to be discovered.

**Two ways into the adapter, and the reference is resolved differently in each — F2's rule, applied.**
For a case of ours the request carries a `DisputeId` and the adapter reads the `CaseNumber` out of
`gateway_references` with `findForDispute()`, throwing when there is no row: the CMS read is addressed
by a reference, and a case of ours with no stored one is our bookkeeping missing, not an empty answer
about the case. For an unattributable case the request was built by
`AvailableActionsRequest::unattributable()` and the provider's own reference **is** the name it
carries — that is the only reason the constructor exists, and the reference is passed through without
ever being stored. The two paths differ in what they may consult as well as in how they are named:
only the attributable one can ask our aggregate whether the case is still waiting on us (F7's
`ReadsDisputeCases` reading is what answers it), and the unattributable one answers from the
provider's rules alone.

**The deep link's base URL is not in the reference** — see the revision note. Take it from
configuration and report which variable must be set; do not derive it from the API host, and do not
let F9's Done-when pass on a link built from a guessed portal origin. What the code owes is the
*shape* (case identity in the query, so an operator lands on the case rather than a search box), and
a test that pins the shape against whatever base it is handed.

**The PDF package builder has no library and no home yet.** Nothing in the tree produces a PDF and no
`composer.json` declares one (`dompdf`, `mpdf`, `tcpdf` — all absent). Adding one is not this task's
to decide: a package's `require` block is real — the packages are split and published by
`bin/split.sh` — so a dependency added there ships to every consumer of that gateway. The two
Done-when clauses below do not mention the PDF, and the portal accepts JPEG as well as PDF, so the
buildable part is the **composition** (which items, in what order, as one file, in what format) and
it is separable from the encoding. Implement the composition, report the encoder as a decision for a
human, and do not hand an operator a file that was never rendered by anything.

`SubmitEvidencePort` is **not** implemented for ConnexPay — there is no API. An operator submits in
the portal; confirmation arrives only through F5 as `HasResponse: true`, which is what moves
`SubmissionState` to `CONFIRMED`. The operator's own "I submitted it" claim is A3's task queue and
reaches at most `SUBMITTED`.

Rationale for the split: with a 12-day deadline, automatic loss on expiry, and $20 already debited,
the cost of treating a checkbox as confirmation is a lost case.

**Done when:** an action-set test shows a non-empty set with one `DashboardDisputeAction` carrying a
working deep link, and a
state test shows `HasResponse: false` on a subsequent poll leaves `SubmissionState::SUBMITTED`
rather than advancing it.

